#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124130 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/id #uuid "3344e23f-56eb-5880-b1cd-7198525daf91" :question/math-academy-id "e-12554" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9  + z^2 = 1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 =  1$ and its boundary.  \n\nTherefore, the domain is of $z = z(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\bigg\\{ (x, y) \\,:\\, \\dfrac {x^2} {4} +  \\dfrac {y^2} 9 \\leq  1 \\bigg\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124226 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/id #uuid "ca62112b-9989-5ede-9cbf-561f5dc2ba43" :question/math-academy-id "e-5263" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\dfrac {(x-2)^2} {2} +  {y^2} + \\dfrac{(z+1)^2}{2} =1,$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+(0)^{2}+\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $\\left(1, 0, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(3, 0, 0\\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124230 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "8db4fc0c-416b-57a4-8612-a422a7681076" :question/math-academy-id "e-5311" :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+1)^2 + y^2 + z^2 + 8z = 20?$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of a sphere centered at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ with radius $r > 0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 + (y-y_0)^2 + (z-z_0)^2 = r^2.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+8z & =20 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+({z}^{2}+8z+16) & =20+16 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+4)^{2} & =36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-1))^2 + (y-0)^2 + (z-(-4))^2 = 6^2,\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point $C(-1,0,-4)$ and the radius $r=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124231 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/id #uuid "ecc934f5-9339-5076-b2d0-58b113364b54" :question/math-academy-id "e-5312" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the sphere $x^2 + (y+2)^2 + (z-3)^2 = 25?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+(0-3)^{2} & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}+9 & =25 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(0,-2)$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dd0610f-d04f-54ac-b5a6-be294f418c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121472} {:answer/id #uuid "c48d55ef-a55e-56a7-8404-61ad05f91c13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(3, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121473} {:answer/id #uuid "d03318a7-a2c6-5f9d-9527-214f8cbc71a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121474} {:answer/id #uuid "651e3eb0-38fb-55cc-a314-8caa69d38ab5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0, 3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121475} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0719c8-4f64-5495-9cf8-ca4bef0bc37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(-3, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dd0610f-d04f-54ac-b5a6-be294f418c71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121472} :answer-field/id #uuid "21c9d27b-7943-552f-8739-696013a2021f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "329288e3-c4cc-54c9-9a15-597f2748e70c" :question/math-academy-id "q-164186" :question/problem "Given the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=2,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =2 \\\\ (z-1)^{2} & =2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =1\\pm \\sqrt{2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}=2\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $(0,-3)$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e40086d5-749b-5222-bec3-9c41b3b87024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315136} {:answer/id #uuid "12c6bf60-308a-5f56-ad36-36c86a67a317" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315137} {:answer/id #uuid "e3327e7d-a655-5711-bc94-23542812678a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,3)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315138} {:answer/id #uuid "c484d389-9eae-5ef9-9f33-c845aac49b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315139} {:answer/id #uuid "2c9e1ab0-15ac-541e-ade7-70cdfb1cfa50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ centered at $(0,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c484d389-9eae-5ef9-9f33-c845aac49b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315139} :answer-field/id #uuid "f600bd91-85b2-5dea-9840-5f1d7258644a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d92bb0fb-ca2e-543b-a67c-2516a62739fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-164216" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipsoid:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{16}+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{{1}^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of the ellipse with semi-axes $4$ and $1,$ centered at $(3,0)$ in the $xz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c28ea49c-006e-5e92-860f-d94069085acf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315143} {:answer/id #uuid "6526dcd7-cf3b-5749-97e9-4f5b36759956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315144} {:answer/id #uuid "6f19da9a-a976-55db-ba1b-0bb043ee255b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(0,0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315145} {:answer/id #uuid "57f9e0dc-6c1d-5479-b4c5-05ab74aff856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315146} {:answer/id #uuid "77546191-1b7f-5baa-874d-a4e9318fb0ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0).$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57f9e0dc-6c1d-5479-b4c5-05ab74aff856" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315146} :answer-field/id #uuid "4080cdc1-f4d2-50fe-88c6-c80150fc91fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8358ba3d-fe0d-5487-9222-565dc3b520a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-164251" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{4} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =2\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=0$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(0,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c62fd80a-e615-5dd7-8d53-16db17180b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-16)$ and $(0,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315150} {:answer/id #uuid "f614a5a2-38f3-5d96-99e4-882716ca9af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,1)$ and $(0,0,16)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315151} {:answer/id #uuid "4f31cdba-31ec-569a-846f-af04267fa44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315152} {:answer/id #uuid "97c74603-0d49-555c-b219-44d83e11abcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-1)$ and $(0,0,16)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315153} {:answer/id #uuid "a4fadf49-25f0-5cec-9649-8fc68af85ff6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f31cdba-31ec-569a-846f-af04267fa44a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315152} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddef1153-9558-5b74-8ad2-f87a678d6347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "013c9984-69bc-55ec-a51a-14e22fa98e0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164275" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(0-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{10}+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{40} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ z & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-4$ and $z=4.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-4)$ and $(0,0,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315155 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa5341f1-ab9b-5010-afbc-cb3a9c297b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315157} {:answer/id #uuid "05ca48e1-0d8a-5b1c-ad56-1176dfcd9483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(1,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315158} {:answer/id #uuid "9a5ee55f-54b8-5d95-9002-173b0aacd687" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315159} {:answer/id #uuid "3420634e-0cfa-577e-bd4a-241ef1a74b7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(3,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315160} {:answer/id #uuid "c18d4fac-d47d-5e9f-be53-772e700e1d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315161}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa5341f1-ab9b-5010-afbc-cb3a9c297b59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315157} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e6f3958-59c5-54f9-9454-66e264b97a30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315156}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e6a384d-7ca2-5551-9c7a-30830f5ff58a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164311" :question/problem "Given the sphere $(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-2)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-2)^{2} & =9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})} \\\\ z & =2\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315162 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e88a93-80f6-5024-b2dc-461333550327" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{10}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315164} {:answer/id #uuid "8ba095ea-13f1-5b07-ab75-d4dbc6b64323" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{5}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{10}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315165} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b2ebd1-e5a2-5282-9a02-34c028bda414" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{10}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315166} {:answer/id #uuid "1b368d08-2ceb-5348-a845-cf4b9252acea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315167} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6568c1-b53c-5bf3-b3bb-7e2fb2363f52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-2)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{10}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315168}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b368d08-2ceb-5348-a845-cf4b9252acea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315167} :answer-field/id #uuid "c01f0dcb-19ec-5895-849e-d01f4f8252c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315163}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "160c2e7f-7903-5bb5-85db-a233663b371d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164317" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}+\\frac{(z+1)^{2}}{5} & =1 \\\\ (z+1)^{2} & =5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}) \\\\ z+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{5(1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}-{y}^{2}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}=1$ and its boundary. \n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315169 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d9ee151-cf6c-5263-b815-85ff5f47aaa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315171} {:answer/id #uuid "85325582-ab19-5c7f-85ec-1bb6f9342717" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-3,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315172} {:answer/id #uuid "aeb079b6-1d5c-5a45-9dbf-30e2e7e32086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315173} {:answer/id #uuid "df9beecc-e2b2-57b5-b50e-05799f8f27f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $9$ centered at $(3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315174} {:answer/id #uuid "4923d341-be85-5b8d-88c9-ee9b2ecf645a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $9$ centered at $(-3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aeb079b6-1d5c-5a45-9dbf-30e2e7e32086" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315173} :answer-field/id #uuid "afdbb149-1654-52a3-99bc-b8b65b44e455" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "05acb460-cd41-56dc-8b5b-f6ebf429381f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164378" :question/problem "Given the sphere $(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-4)^{2}=9,$ what is the domain of $z=z(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "From the equation of the sphere, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+(z-4)^{2} & =9 \\\\ (z-4)^{2} & =9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}) \\\\ z-4 & =\\pm \\sqrt{9-({x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2})} \\\\ z & =4\\pm \\sqrt{9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-((x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2})\\ge 0 \\\\ (x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}\\le 9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis inequality describes the interior and boundary of the circle \n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}=9\n$$\n\nwhich is a circle of radius $3$ centered at $(-3,1)$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c2d581-8b12-575d-bb78-d8bb293a7339" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Sphere or Ellipsoid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d350195-56fa-5618-9340-8f72c43ae52b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}<1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315177} {:answer/id #uuid "8c5371f3-da5d-5f75-b98e-a511ee8ef652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315178} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6fc4f0-0737-5b25-a001-f06377fa20df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315179} {:answer/id #uuid "4d23800d-ef58-50dc-ad3e-973a943b148d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Entire $xy$-plane" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315180} {:answer/id #uuid "ee15b1b4-eae5-553c-adf7-517e7687dfe1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}>1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c5371f3-da5d-5f75-b98e-a511ee8ef652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315178} :answer-field/id #uuid "25473d56-1170-5a5d-91e6-08b4fd49f7d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315176}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6e03d2b-cbce-5eba-bc2e-8ab166e28ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-35518" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "From the ellipsoid equation, we make $z$ the subject as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}+(z+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (z+2)^{2} & =1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ z+2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the square root is defined only for non-negative arguments, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\ge 0 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the interior of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1$ and its boundary.\n\nTherefore, the domain is \n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}\\le 1}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bcca99e-b125-5421-9bbd-d72f051d9e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r = \\sqrt{2}$ and $C(0,-3, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123791} {:answer/id #uuid "bdb5d6c6-5532-532c-b171-64bd224d3ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r = 2$ and $C(0,-3, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123792} {:answer/id #uuid "02f63c3a-4747-5032-9382-63c04f627840" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r = 2$ and $C(0, 3, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123793} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce53bf7-5e08-526a-83f3-597c609921df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r = \\sqrt{2}$ and $C(0, 3,-1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123794} {:answer/id #uuid "63d9f161-8cc5-5ca9-a9ff-1f85846640fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r = \\sqrt{2}$ and $C(0, 3, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bcca99e-b125-5421-9bbd-d72f051d9e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r = \\sqrt{2}$ and $C(0,-3, 1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123791} :answer-field/id #uuid "effa39fa-ae42-5659-8553-68a27a51386d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdb4f6bd-92de-5fb3-a5cc-3d88dfc3947f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70560" :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=2?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can write our equation as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y-(-3))^{2}+(z-1)^{2}=(\\sqrt{2})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point point $C(0,-3,1)$ and the radius $r=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85b4a262-2dfa-545d-99b0-9a8098ee12da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123797} {:answer/id #uuid "9e1ca5d5-b103-5e35-aacf-8fed62afc9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123798} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4ee2a4-3b1f-5187-993f-fd0e0209b3b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123799} {:answer/id #uuid "d4479bf3-09c8-5027-8141-b323a5ce2411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123800} {:answer/id #uuid "430492e0-c55c-58c8-bfdc-6e52f47d06f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e1ca5d5-b103-5e35-aacf-8fed62afc9d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123798} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d481463-3352-51bd-bc7f-abf6b5aad3db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6b356f6b-9965-5431-ba35-16fc873edc26" :question/math-academy-id "q-70563" :question/problem "What is the radius of the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+(z-1)^{2}=16?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $y$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+6y+(z-1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {x}^{2}+({y}^{2}+6y+9)+(z-1)^{2} & =16+9 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2} & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y+3)^{2}+(z-1)^{2}={5}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a radius of $5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bca39e2-4243-5f92-b5f9-1bcc581272b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=4$ and $C(-2,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315184} {:answer/id #uuid "39823bf7-f9d5-5564-9f1a-5d08d914507a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=2$ and $C(-2,3,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315185} {:answer/id #uuid "e88a2693-94fb-5c35-96b1-c6c797d7904b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=4$ and $C(0,2,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315186} {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd5537-3e50-531d-9634-90349ea00827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=2$ and $C(-2,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315187} {:answer/id #uuid "95986a67-9578-502e-a533-7f8cab914344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=2$ and $C(2,0,-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1cd5537-3e50-531d-9634-90349ea00827" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=2$ and $C(-2,0,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315187} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e7a5e35-6cab-5067-aaef-5001067da37e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed5f8796-f85d-5746-b3cc-e0baddec1bbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-70648" :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere $(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2}=4?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x-(-2))^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2}={2}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point point $C(-2,0,3)$ and the radius $r=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315189 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108222d8-d2c5-50bc-8d03-5c3b1d39f906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=\\sqrt{10}$ and $C(0,5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315191} {:answer/id #uuid "366e1560-68b7-5587-a581-fe270f884f2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=10$ and $C(0,0,-5)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315192} {:answer/id #uuid "473c217e-79be-50a8-b2c7-ec8dfe9b9dbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=10$ and $C(0,5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315193} {:answer/id #uuid "9463f20b-6054-5312-9bf3-f9c30931efe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=\\sqrt{10}$ and $C(-5,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315194} {:answer/id #uuid "815e6098-0e17-561c-b331-0a88524a0607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=\\sqrt{10}$ and $C(0,-5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "815e6098-0e17-561c-b331-0a88524a0607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$r=\\sqrt{10}$ and $C(0,-5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315195} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1a1d4ba-3b0a-5720-8ca0-52ca1cb60f0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315190}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69541963-bfeb-5f91-b675-aa9309c1e160" :question/math-academy-id "q-70649" :question/problem "What are the radius $r$ and the coordinates of the center $C$ for the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+5)^{2}+{z}^{2}=10?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can write our equation as\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+(y-(-5))^{2}+{z}^{2}=(\\sqrt{10})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us the center at the point point $C(0,-5,0)$ and the radius $r=\\sqrt{10}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "304a85ac-e980-5081-8f2b-20df471c30fc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Center and Radius of a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57aeb756-9c5f-536c-98e2-a76517d566e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315198} {:answer/id #uuid "f8128909-66c7-5afe-8596-fc67fe12f8cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315199} {:answer/id #uuid "384006ed-8187-5e6d-a37c-304da48e4136" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315200} {:answer/id #uuid "b8ecb18d-33aa-5f20-bb98-ef68a955bbc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315201} {:answer/id #uuid "3f326182-ca89-5d36-8b2a-be56f652957a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8ecb18d-33aa-5f20-bb98-ef68a955bbc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315201} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b465e7f-7999-5afe-bb46-dbb70c1c98c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "361d563b-06bb-5688-be7a-668acbcfa976" :question/math-academy-id "q-70650" :question/problem "What is the radius of the sphere ${x}^{2}+4x+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2}=12?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of a sphere centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ with radius $r>0$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-{x}_{0})^{2}+(y-{y}_{0})^{2}+(z-{z}_{0})^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nBy completing the square for the variable $x$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+4x+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2} & =12 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+4x+4)+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2} & =12+4 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can write our equation as\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z-3)^{2}={4}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a radius of $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1347e99-63f2-58bc-bb94-35d775e1d32d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315205} {:answer/id #uuid "4b06f284-5ac1-5aab-9668-4b5a71bfd5b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $12$ centered at $(0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315206} {:answer/id #uuid "35f3b4d2-4341-5a24-aad2-d77468677527" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315207} {:answer/id #uuid "9021a18e-8f13-54df-8c5d-4781a03f2dae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $4$ centered at $(4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315208} {:answer/id #uuid "a2e249bd-6e6c-5c99-bebf-93aa09ea6d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $2\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2e249bd-6e6c-5c99-bebf-93aa09ea6d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $2\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-4,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315209} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a888bc-747b-5c3d-ae12-7ce3342fe3a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1624d2db-d10f-59ae-bae3-c241890eb9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-70926" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+{z}^{2}=16$ and the plane $x=2$ onto the $yz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the sphere and the plane $x=2$, we substitute $x=2$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ 4+(y+4)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =12 \\\\ (y+4)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $yz$-plane gives us the circle of radius $2\\sqrt{3}$ centered at $(-4,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83c678c9-12a0-5e35-9400-b5750d5e01d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + (z - 2)^{2} = 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123803} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b5d2a8-d1d1-5c3e-bd8f-4dd212745974" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (y + 2)^{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123804} {:answer/id #uuid "ca93f32c-7c9a-58b6-81fd-7561e3926509" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y^{2} + (z + 2)^{2} = 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123805} {:answer/id #uuid "482877d7-843e-5dd9-9506-407eaacea96b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + z^{2} = 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123806} {:answer/id #uuid "6c113339-b391-5978-b0bc-464fde651daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (z + 2)^{2} = 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c113339-b391-5978-b0bc-464fde651daa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x - 2)^{2} + (z + 2)^{2} = 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123807} :answer-field/id #uuid "be70486c-919c-538b-a051-d29784aeae04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "863e5eab-b375-5fa8-afc8-dc170a1abcfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-70927" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the sphere $(x-2)^{2}+{y}^{2}+(z+2)^{2}=16?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xz$-plane, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(z+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(z+2)^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315210 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d563285b-f63c-569d-b098-93c69288680c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,\\frac{11}{6})$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315212} {:answer/id #uuid "df166535-abb6-5754-9b30-14b9515c509e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,0,\\frac{11}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315213} {:answer/id #uuid "d095a839-e3d2-5dea-a229-9ef40be25094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-\\frac{11}{6})$ and $(0,0,\\frac{11}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315214} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fabc7d-c617-50a8-933a-8bda692196f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315215} {:answer/id #uuid "57b14a7b-dd6e-553c-9c26-5c7dc839b29f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-\\sqrt{11})$ and $(0,0,\\sqrt{11})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d095a839-e3d2-5dea-a229-9ef40be25094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,-\\frac{11}{6})$ and $(0,0,\\frac{11}{6})$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315214} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4d2658-5b5a-5aed-aa5e-9322ded6faf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315211}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d29c492-0ca8-561c-9ff3-546ae9bcb878" :question/math-academy-id "q-71118" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $z$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $y=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{4}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{z}^{2}}{11} & =\\frac{11}{36} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =\\frac{121}{36} \\\\ z & =\\pm \\frac{11}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $z=-\\frac{11}{6}$ and $z=\\frac{11}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $z$-axis are $(0,0,-\\frac{11}{6})$ and $(0,0,\\frac{11}{6}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "828031ca-cf8f-5013-84f3-a3fa904ea0d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $16$ centered at $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315219} {:answer/id #uuid "58ee27a9-cece-5e0f-8577-c8301ab9bafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $4$ centered at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315220} {:answer/id #uuid "eef654be-93f0-5f0b-ac8b-3c15be8a64db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $4$ centered at $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315221} {:answer/id #uuid "8574b591-1336-5f46-9cc6-be7de780b477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $\\sqrt{17}$ centered at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315222} {:answer/id #uuid "c18c4038-22ef-53ad-9173-b34d218312e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $16$ centered at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58ee27a9-cece-5e0f-8577-c8301ab9bafb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $4$ centered at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315220} :answer-field/id #uuid "1597ca5b-9f01-547b-8d92-8a16ebd8b92f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d8b4045-9642-5938-bbc6-e68bf8139eaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-71948" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the sphere $(x-1)^{2}+(y-6)^{2}+{z}^{2}=17$ and the plane $y=5$ onto the $xz$-plane." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the sphere and the plane $y=5$, we substitute $y=5$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2}+(5-6)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =17 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+1+{z}^{2} & =17 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & ={4}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $xz$-plane gives us the equation of the circle of radius $4$ centered at $(1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8db2909b-26fd-53bf-8ce2-9fca4451efc3" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Traces of Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "829ab927-6d2a-5df2-aa53-fb0c7abc757c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $2\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315226} {:answer/id #uuid "feb0d2fa-2a6a-5a65-8854-95ac65518a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $2\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315227} {:answer/id #uuid "99a725f7-be8e-50bf-be65-c206e4da2af9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $20$ centered at $(0,-2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315228} {:answer/id #uuid "9f8bf85f-e24d-5186-bc2a-6f8b329cb4fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $6$ centered at $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315229} {:answer/id #uuid "9a86893e-38bc-5e8c-b770-371902eb9eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315230}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "feb0d2fa-2a6a-5a65-8854-95ac65518a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The circle of radius $2\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(0,2)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315227} :answer-field/id #uuid "da0094fd-2e79-546e-8474-d026140c413b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315225}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d2ff6712-19ec-54c8-bda1-46ab6fc386b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-71949" :question/problem "What is the projection of the intersection between the sphere ${x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(z+1)^{2}=36$ and the plane $z=3$ onto the $xy$-plane." :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the sphere and the plane $z=3$, we substitute $z=3$ into the equation of the sphere:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+(3+1)^{2} & =36 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2}+16 & =36 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =(2\\sqrt{5})^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nProjecting this onto the $xy$-plane gives us the equation of the circle of radius $2\\sqrt{5}$ centered at $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315231 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1eb96635-4614-5a23-8bca-1f41d63ed2c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,0,0)$ and $(2,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315233} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c06e65-98d2-5e45-a44c-9fd61df94696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315234} {:answer/id #uuid "4c49531e-882a-5825-9917-3231652dd4fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-4,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315235} {:answer/id #uuid "0fba62fc-af7e-5b7c-92b4-67530b459b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(4,0,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315236} {:answer/id #uuid "f63bc0f1-3bd8-5ca5-ab72-36dbf9250031" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3\\sqrt{2},0,0)$ and $(3\\sqrt{2},0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3c06e65-98d2-5e45-a44c-9fd61df94696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315234} :answer-field/id #uuid "16d75cdf-6f3a-5212-bef0-1af4bed23b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315232}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc3f69db-faba-5b23-aeec-3cea350fe1f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-73190" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $x$-intercepts, we plug $y=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{(0+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{0}^{2}}{16} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18}+\\frac{1}{2}+0 & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{18} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =9 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 3 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $x=4$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $x$-axis are $(-2,0,0)$ and $(4,0,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125133 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "64e7d2fb-003c-58c0-ab80-159e50748cc7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Intercepts of Spheres and Ellipsoids"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89d896af-1b14-5807-8275-0406d713a6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{8}{3}, 0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3}, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123821} {:answer/id #uuid "d049c7ac-c56b-5ea2-9598-7c65565ce0c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0, 0, 0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3}, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123822} {:answer/id #uuid "dddf154f-1b73-5e42-a493-43ef77ae5b37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-2, 0)$ and $(0, 2, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123823} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a807ca-9a06-51d9-8a74-3ae1b5177630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{16}{9}, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123824} {:answer/id #uuid "75dbceb0-42fd-526e-a4a5-8bca163e9bc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{4}{3}, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89d896af-1b14-5807-8275-0406d713a6b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(0,-\\frac{8}{3}, 0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3}, 0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123821} :answer-field/id #uuid "e12e18a8-8baf-55e0-9d72-e7a712e428d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92b5bb6f-415d-5913-8624-6ebb244cd292" :question/math-academy-id "q-73214" :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts, we plug $x=0$ and $z=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(0+1)^{2}}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{(0-2)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{9}+(y+2)^{2}+\\frac{4}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{5}{9}+(y+2)^{2} & =1 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ y & =-2\\pm \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the solutions $y=-\\frac{8}{3}$ and $y=-\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the intercepts with the $y$-axis are $(0,-\\frac{8}{3},0)$ and $(0,-\\frac{4}{3},0).$"}]}
